@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+---
+id: dl-004-json-patch-diff-strategy-for-createpatch
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Patch diff strategy for createPatch"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 6902 defines how a JSON Patch is applied, but not how one is produced from two documents; there is
+no standard to defer to, unlike `resolvePointer` (dl-001), `applyPatch` (dl-002) and `applyMergePatch`
+(dl-003). `createPatch` is built on `applyPatch`'s existing operation set and on `resolvePointer`'s
+reference-token escaping, so the diff only needs to decide what operations to emit, not how they
+behave once applied. The request's acceptance bar is "describe what changed, and nothing more" — not
+a globally minimal patch, since that would require a full edit-distance search over arbitrary
+reorderings.
+
+## Decision
+
+- **Recursive structural diff, not edit-distance minimization**: two values are compared by shape —
+  both plain objects, both arrays, or neither — and the smaller of those three cases is handled
+  specifically; anything else (a type change, or two unequal scalars) becomes a single `replace` at
+  that path. This keeps the diff linear in document size, at the cost of not detecting moves: an
+  element relocated within an array or an object is emitted as a remove-plus-add/replace pair, not a
+  `move` operation. Given the request's examples (a changed field, not a reordering), this trade-off
+  favours predictability over a smaller byte count in the rare case a move would be shorter.
+- **Objects diff by key membership, then recurse per shared key**: a key present in `from` but absent
+  from `to` becomes `remove`; a key present in `to` but absent from `from` becomes `add` with a
+  deep-cloned value; a key present in both recurses structurally instead of always emitting `replace`,
+  so a change nested arbitrarily deep produces one operation at the deepest differing path, not a
+  `replace` of the whole containing object.
+- **Arrays diff by common-prefix index, not by longest-common-subsequence**: indices `0` up to
+  `min(from.length, to.length) - 1` are compared pairwise and recursed into; any extra trailing `from`
+  indices are removed (highest index first, so earlier removals never invalidate later ones); any
+  extra trailing `to` indices are appended with `add` (lowest index first, matching how each insertion
+  shifts the array that `applyPatch` is replaying against). This correctly produces a minimal patch for
+  the common case of appending/truncating a tail or changing an element in place, but — like the
+  object case above — does not detect an insertion/deletion in the middle of an array, which would
+  shift every later index and read as a wall of per-index `replace` operations instead of one `add`.
+  An LCS-based array diff would handle that case too, but was judged unwarranted complexity for a
+  first version given no caller has asked for it yet.
+- **Root pointer (`""`) as a replace target**: when `from` and `to` are both scalars, or differ in
+  kind at the top level (e.g. an object becoming an array), the patch is a single
+  `[{ op: "replace", path: "", value: to }]`, consistent with dl-002's decision that `replace` at the
+  root pointer replaces the whole document.
+- **Emitted values are deep-cloned, mirroring `applyPatch` and `applyMergePatch`**: every `value` an
+  operation carries (`add`, `replace`) is `structuredClone`d from `to` before being placed in the
+  patch, so the returned patch shares no mutable reference with `to`, and neither `from` nor `to` is
+  read destructively. This is the same "must not mutate its input" guarantee dl-002 and dl-003 already
+  give, applied to a function whose output is a patch rather than a document.
+- **Key order follows `Object.keys`, not a sorted or declared order**: removes are emitted in `from`'s
+  key order, adds and recursions in `to`'s key order. This satisfies the `determinism` directive
+  (no reliance on unordered iteration) because `Object.keys` order on plain objects is itself
+  deterministic (insertion order per the spec), not because the output is sorted; two calls with the
+  same inputs always produce byte-identical patches.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers get a patch proportional to the size of the actual change for the common cases (field edits,
+  tail append/truncate on arrays, added/removed object members), satisfying "sending the whole document
+  again is not acceptable" without the complexity of a general diff/edit-distance algorithm.
+- A rename, reorder, or middle-of-array insertion/deletion produces a larger patch than a human would
+  write by hand (remove+add instead of move, or a cascade of replaces instead of one insert). If a
+  future caller needs byte-minimal patches for those cases, that requires a move-detection or
+  LCS-based pass layered on top of (or instead of) this algorithm — a deliberate scope cut, not an
+  oversight.
+- `createPatch(from, to)` composed with `applyPatch(from, patch)` is guaranteed to reproduce `to`
+  (deep-equal), but `createPatch` is not guaranteed to be the unique or shortest patch doing so.
